meta {
  name: Get Audit Workflow
  type: http
  seq: 12
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/deal-audits/deals/{{auditDealId}}
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("returns the workflow state with owners resolved", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.have.property("auditStatus");
    expect(body).to.have.property("assignee");
    expect(body).to.have.property("reviewer");
    expect(body).to.have.property("completionPct");
  });

  test("tells the caller what they may do", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.canSubmit).to.be.a("boolean");
    expect(body.canReview).to.be.a("boolean");
  });

  test("never reveals who submitted it", function () {
    // `canReview` already encodes the submitter check. Exposing the id purely
    // so the UI could compare it would leak an identity for no other reason.
    expect(res.getBody()).to.not.have.property("submittedById");
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /deal-audits/deals/:dealId — the audit's workflow state (PAC-72)

  What the hand-off drawer opens with: who owns the audit, where it sits in the
  review workflow, and **what the caller may do next**.

  - **Module:** `deal_audits`
  - **Permission:** `deal_audits:read`
  - **Data scope:** assignee **or** reviewer (see **Set Audit Assignment**).

  Read separately from the board rather than folded into every row: resolving
  the two owners' display names costs an extra lookup each, and the board
  renders eight cards of which the user opens one.

  ## Response `200`
  ```json
  {
    "id": "665f...9c1",
    "dealId": "665f...a03",
    "auditStatus": "Pending",
    "assignee": { "type": "user", "id": "665f...b11", "name": "Pat Producer" },
    "reviewer": { "type": "role", "id": "665f...ab2", "name": "CRM" },
    "submittedAt": "2026-08-21T10:04:00.000Z",
    "reviewedAt": null,
    "reasonCodes": [],
    "auditScore": 0,
    "auditNotes": null,
    "itemCount": 7,
    "resolvedCount": 5,
    "openCount": 2,
    "completionPct": 71,
    "canSubmit": false,
    "canReview": true
  }
  ```

  ## `canSubmit` / `canReview`
  Decided by the **server**, and the UI gates its buttons on them rather than
  re-deriving the rules.

  `canReview` depends on who submitted — the submitter may not review their own
  audit — and `submittedById` is deliberately **not** in the response. Returning
  it purely so a client could make the comparison would leak an identity for no
  other reason, and would let two implementations of the rule drift apart.

  ## Errors
  - `403 Forbidden` — module disabled or missing `deal_audits:read`.
  - `404 Not Found` — no audit for that deal, or out of the caller's reach.
}
